Experiment: Enumeration & Password Cracking using Hashcat / John the Ripper (Kali Linux)
🎯 Aim

To perform password cracking on hashed data using Hashcat (or John the Ripper) in Kali Linux.

🎯 Objectives
To understand password hashing concepts
To perform enumeration of hashed passwords
To crack passwords using dictionary attacks
To analyze password security weaknesses
🧠 Concept (Short for Viva)
Passwords are stored as hashes, not plain text
Hashing is a one-way function
Password cracking = guessing input → matching hash

Tools:

Hashcat → GPU-based (fast)
John the Ripper → CPU-based (reliable fallback)
⚙️ Tools Used
4
Hashcat
John the Ripper
rockyou wordlist
🧾 Requirements
Kali Linux
Hash file
Wordlist (rockyou.txt)
🪜 Procedure (Step-by-Step)
🔹 Step 1: Create Hash File
echo "5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99" > hash.txt

👉 (MD5 hash of "password")

🔹 Step 2: Prepare Wordlist
sudo gunzip /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt.gz
🔹 Step 3: Attempt Password Cracking using Hashcat
hashcat -m 0 -a 0 hash.txt /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt

👉 Note: Hashcat requires GPU/OpenCL support

🔹 Step 4: Use John the Ripper (Fallback Method)
john --format=raw-md5 --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt hash.txt
🔹 Step 5: Display Cracked Password
john --show --format=raw-md5 hash.txt

👉 Output:

password
👀 Observations
Hash file was successfully created
Dictionary attack was performed using wordlist
Hashcat could not run due to lack of GPU/OpenCL support
John the Ripper was used as an alternative
Password was successfully cracked
Recovered password: password
✅ Result

The password hash was successfully cracked using a dictionary attack. The experiment demonstrated how weak passwords can be easily recovered using password cracking techniques.
